🌳 Binary Tree Representation (Topper Style Explanation)

A Binary Tree is a hierarchical data structure in which each node has at most two children — called the left child and the right child.

1️⃣ Definition

A Binary Tree is a finite set of nodes that is either:

Empty (NULL), or

Consists of a root node and two disjoint binary trees called the left subtree and the right subtree.

2️⃣ Structure of a Binary Tree Node

Each node typically contains:

Data (value stored in the node)

Pointer to left child

Pointer to right child

📘 In C++ representation:

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* left;
    Node* right;

    Node(int val) {
        data = val;
        left = right = NULL;
    }
};

3️⃣ Types of Representation
(a) Linked Representation

Each node contains data and two pointers (left and right).

The root pointer points to the topmost node of the tree.

📘 Example:

Node* root = new Node(10);
root->left = new Node(20);
root->right = new Node(30);


📊 Structure:

       10
      /  \
    20    30


✅ Advantages:

Dynamic in nature.

Easy to insert and delete nodes.
